{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>There shall be an inquiry.</b> The court must investigate the matter so as not to impose the death penalty upon her as in the law of a married woman, even though she is designated <b>for she was not freed</b> — that is, so as to be his fully married wife. And this is the meaning of חרופה לאיש — designated to him and set aside for him for intercourse, but not as a fully married wife. And similarly the meaning of בקר is that a person can examine and distinguish between the forms, whereas ערב is their opposite, in which the forms become mixed. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: <b>there shall be an inquiry</b> — it shall be בקראי, meaning, she shall be flogged, for the judges who administer lashes would call out to the one being flogged; and from here we learn that she is subject to lashes but he is not, and therefore it says בקרת תהיה in the singular and does not say יהיו, for he is not included — thus did our Rabbis of blessed memory interpret. And the reason is that Scripture already imposed upon him an offering, as it says ״והביד את אשמו״, and this is what we learned: the asham-offering of a designated maidservavnt applies to him; but she, since she is a maidservant and has nothing with which to bring an offering, is flogged." "<b>And in the name of</b> the Gaon, our master Sa'adyah, of blessed memory, they said that Scripture mentioned lashes in the language of בקרת because the lashes are administered with a strap of cattle-hide (בקר); or the letter bet may interchange with the letter peh, meaning that she was הפקר (ownerless / unrestricted), because she was not freed, and therefore they shall not be put to death, but she is subject to lashes."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:04.175463827Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}